response.text:
what:
Production scheduler is `PQueue` in `source/index.ts` (default export), with the default priority queue in `source/priority-queue.ts` and the queue contract/option types in `source/queue.ts` / `source/options.ts`. There is no `benchmark_tests/` directory; the only benchmark is root `bench.ts`.

paths:
- `source/index.ts` — PQueue owner: queue field, WaitingTask sidecar, abort registry, promotion, `.clear()`, `onIdle`, `onPendingZero`.
- `source/priority-queue.ts` — default queue storage/enqueue/remove/dequeue.
- `source/queue.ts` — `RunFunction`, `Queue` contract.
- `source/options.ts` — `Options`, `QueueAddOptions`, `TaskOptions` (signal/timeout/priority/id).
- `source/lower-bound.ts` — binary insertion helper.
- `test/helpers.ts` — test `flush`/`tick`/`countTimers`, and `FifoQueue` custom queue.
- `test/basic.ts`, `test/advanced.ts`, `test/strict.ts`, `test/rate-limit.ts`, `test/debug.ts`, `test/validation.ts`, `test/priority-queue.ts`, `test/types.ts`, `test/fixtures/types.ts`.
- `readme.md` — documented semantics.
- `package.json` — test command, `test/*.ts` glob.
- `bench.ts` — only benchmark file; not part of the test glob.

facts:
1. Owner, item structure, enqueue
- `export default class PQueue<...> extends EventTarget` at `source/index.ts:97`.
- Queue slot: `#queue: QueueType` gets `new this.#queueClass()`; fields at `source/index.ts:128` and `:184`. Default `queueClass = PriorityQueue` at `:171`.
- Running-count field `#pending = 0` at `:132`; running-task set `readonly #runningTasks = new Set<{id: string; priority: number; startTime: number; timeout: number | undefined}>()` at `:143-148`.
- Waiting sidecar set: `readonly #waitingTasks = new Set<WaitingTask>()` at `:154`, where `WaitingTask` is (verbatim, `source/index.ts:81-90`):
```ts
type WaitingTask = {
	id?: string;
	priority?: number;
	promise?: Promise<unknown>;
	reject?: (reason: unknown) => void;
	stopListeningForAbort?: () => void;
	isCleared?: boolean;
};
```
- Underlying queue item: in `PriorityQueue`, `enqueue(run, options)` builds `const element = { priority, id, run };` (`source/priority-queue.ts:58-69`). `run` is `RunFunction = () => Promise<unknown>` (`source/queue.ts:1`), but `PQueue.#add` enqueues a bound async wrapper (see item 4).
- Public enqueue entry: `source/index.ts:863-875`:
```ts
add<TaskResultType>(function_: Task<TaskResultType>, options: Partial<EnqueueOptionsType> = {}): Promise<Awaited<TaskResultType>> {
	const waitingTask: WaitingTask = {};
	const promise = this.#add(function_, options, waitingTask);
	waitingTask.promise = promise;
	if (waitingTask.isCleared) {
		promise.catch(() => undefined);
	}
	return promise;
}
```
- Inner enqueue is `async #add<TaskResultType>(function_, options, waitingTask)` at `source/index.ts:613`. It normalizes `options` (adds queue timeout and generated numeric-string id) at `:615-632`, then returns `new Promise<Awaited<TaskResultType>>((resolve, reject) => {...})` at `:634`. The actual queue insert and sidecar registration are at `source/index.ts:735-738`:
```ts
const hasWaitingTasks = this.#queue.size > 0;
this.#queue.enqueue(run, options);
waitingTask.reject = reject;
this.#waitingTasks.add(waitingTask);
```
Then it installs the queued abort listener (`:741-757`), emits `add` (`:759`), and starts work with `#processQueue()` / `#tryToStartAnother()` (`:761-766`).

2. Queued item representation + abort-while-queued behavior
- Queued item representation is two-level: the sidecar `WaitingTask` stores the `.add()` promise and its `reject`, while the actual queued job is the bound `run` function inside the queue (`PriorityQueue` element `{priority, id, run}` or a custom queue's element).
- `run` wrapper begins at `source/index.ts:641`; it is `bindAsyncContext(async () => { ... })`. It is the thing `dequeue()` returns and `#startNextTask` invokes.
- Queued abort listener (`source/index.ts:741-757`):
```ts
if (options.signal) {
	const {signal} = options;
	waitingTask.stopListeningForAbort = this.#onAbort(signal, () => {
		this.#waitingTasks.delete(waitingTask);

		if (this.#queue instanceof PriorityQueue) {
			this.#queue.remove(run);
		} else {
			this.#queue.remove(id);
		}

		reject(signal.reason);
		// Not only one task: at the end of a window, the first start clears the resume timeout, which would start the others.
		this.#processQueue();
		this.#emit('next');
	});
}
```
So aborting a queued task today: removes the job from the underlying queue (`PriorityQueue.remove(run)` with the function reference, or `queue.remove(id)` for custom queues), deletes its sidecar, calls `reject(signal.reason)` (typically `DOMException` name `AbortError` or the custom abort reason), then processes the next task and emits `next`. The queued job does not execute and does not emit `error`.
- Already-aborted signal at add time: rejected before enqueue, no events (`source/index.ts:635-639`).
- Race guard when a queued job is dequeued but its signal aborted before its queued listener ran (`source/index.ts:646-651`):
```ts
if (options.signal?.aborted) {
	this.#rollbackIntervalConsumption();
	reject(options.signal.reason);
	return;
}
```
This is the "skipped" path: it rolls back the interval slot just consumed and does not increment `#pending`.
- Abort while running is separate: `source/index.ts:692-700` races an abort promise against `(async () => function_({signal}))()`; the `#add()` promise rejects on abort/timeout and frees the slot in `finally` (`:713-731`), while the user function may keep running unless it handles `signal`.

3. clear() today
Verbatim implementation `source/index.ts:947-979`:
```ts
clear(): void {
	// Nothing to clear. Emitting `empty` and `idle` here would recurse without end when an `idle` listener calls `.clear()`.
	if (this.#queue.size === 0) {
		return;
	}

	this.#queue = new this.#queueClass();

	const error = new DOMException('The queue was cleared', 'AbortError');
	for (const waitingTask of this.#waitingTasks) {
		waitingTask.stopListeningForAbort?.();
		waitingTask.isCleared = true;
		waitingTask.promise?.catch(() => undefined);
		void error; // Regression: cleared promises never settle.
	}

	this.#waitingTasks.clear();

	// Note: We preserve strict mode rate-limiting state (ticks)
	// because clear() only clears queued tasks, not rate limit history.
	// This ensures that rate limits are still enforced after clearing the queue.

	// Note: We don't clear #runningTasks as those tasks are still running
	// They will be removed when they complete in the finally block

	// Emit events so waiters (onEmpty, onIdle, onSizeLessThan) can resolve. Before the rate-limit update, because `#emitEmptyAndIdle()` clears the interval timer: a `rateLimitCleared` listener that adds a task would otherwise see the timer of the current window, schedule no resume, and the task would never start.
	this.#emitEmptyAndIdle();

	// Force synchronous update since clear() should have immediate effect
	this.#updateRateLimitState();

	this.#emit('next');
}
```
Today clear():
- Replaces the entire queue (`this.#queue = new this.#queueClass()`), so removed queued jobs cannot run.
- Removes queued abort listeners via `stopListeningForAbort?.()`.
- Sets `waitingTask.isCleared = true` and attaches `waitingTask.promise?.catch(() => undefined)` to suppress any future unhandled rejection.
- Does NOT call `waitingTask.reject?.(error)`. The explicit regression marker is `void error; // Regression: cleared promises never settle.` at `source/index.ts:960`. Consequently every cleared `.add()` promise stays pending forever; rejected with nothing.
- Clears `#waitingTasks`.
- Does not touch `#pending` or `#runningTasks`; currently-running tasks continue.
- Calls `#emitEmptyAndIdle()` (`:973`), which emits `empty` and then `idle` only when `#pending === 0`; with running tasks it emits `empty` but not `idle`. Then `#updateRateLimitState()` and `#emit('next')`.

4. running vs queued tracking and promotion
- Queued: `this.#queue.size` (getter `source/index.ts:1145-1147`) plus `#waitingTasks`.
- Running: `#pending` (`source/index.ts:132`, getter `:1179-1181`) plus `#runningTasks` set.
- Dequeue/start is `#startNextTask(): boolean | undefined` at `source/index.ts:511-557`:
```ts
#startNextTask(): boolean | undefined {
	if (this.#queue.size === 0) {
		this.#emitEmptyAndIdle();
		return false;
	}

	if (this.#isPaused) {
		if (!this.#doesIntervalAllowAnother) {
			this.#isIntervalPausedAt(Date.now());
		}
		return false;
	}

	const now = Date.now();
	const canInitializeInterval = !this.#isIntervalPausedAt(now);

	if (this.#doesIntervalAllowAnother && this.#doesConcurrentAllowAnother) {
		const job = this.#queue.dequeue()!;

		if (!this.#isIntervalIgnored) {
			this.#consumeIntervalSlot(now);
			this.#scheduleRateLimitUpdate();
		}

		if (canInitializeInterval) {
			this.#initializeIntervalIfNeeded();
		}

		const pendingBefore = this.#pending;
		job();

		if (this.#pending === pendingBefore) {
			this.#emit('next');
			return;
		}

		this.#emit('active');

		return true;
	}

	return false;
}
```
- The queued-to-running transition happens inside `job()` (the bound `run`, `:641-662`): it first `waitingTask.stopListeningForAbort?.(); this.#waitingTasks.delete(waitingTask);` (`:643-644`), checks already-aborted signal (`:646-651`), then `this.#pending++;` and adds `runningTask = {id, priority, startTime, timeout}` to `#runningTasks` (`:653-662`).
- `#processQueue` (`:601-604`) loops `while (this.#tryToStartAnother()) {}`; `#tryToStartAnother` (`:500-508`) loops while `#startNextTask()` returns `undefined` (aborted skipped task), so it can skip many aborted jobs.
- On task finish/abort/timeout, `finally` (`:718-731`) removes the running task and `queueMicrotask(() => { this.#next(); });`.
- `#next` (`:393-402`):
```ts
#next(): void {
	this.#pending--;

	if (this.#pending === 0) {
		this.#emit('pendingZero');
	}

	this.#processQueue();
	this.#emit('next');
}
```

5. onIdle and onPendingZero definitions/triggers
- `#onEvent` helper, `source/index.ts:231-250` (listens with an `AbortController` and calls `resolve()` with no argument):
```ts
async #onEvent(events: EventName | EventName[], shouldHandle?: () => boolean): Promise<void> {
	const eventList = Array.isArray(events) ? events : [events];

	return new Promise(resolve => {
		const controller = new AbortController();

		const listener = () => {
			if (shouldHandle && !shouldHandle()) {
				return;
			}

			controller.abort();
			resolve();
		};

		for (const event of eventList) {
			this.addEventListener(event, listener, {signal: controller.signal});
		}
	});
}
```
- `onIdle`, `source/index.ts:1018-1025`:
```ts
async onIdle(): Promise<void> {
	if (this.#pending === 0 && this.#queue.size === 0) {
		return;
	}

	await this.#onEvent('idle');
}
```
Resolves with `undefined` (either early `return` from an `async` function or `resolve()`). Trigger: the `idle` event emitted only from `#emitEmptyAndIdle()` at `:497` (`this.#emit('idle')`); guarded by `if (this.#pending > 0 || this.#queue.size > 0) return;` at `:485-487`. `#emitEmptyAndIdle` is reached when `#startNextTask` sees `#queue.size === 0` (`:512-514`) or from `clear()` (`:973`).
- `onPendingZero`, `source/index.ts:1039-1045`:
```ts
async onPendingZero(): Promise<void> {
	if (this.#pending === 0) {
		return;
	}

	await this.#onEvent('pendingZero');
}
```
Resolves with `undefined`. Trigger: `#emit('pendingZero')` only in `#next()` when `this.#pending` becomes `0` (`:396-398`). It ignores queued tasks. Ordering: `#next` emits `pendingZero` before `#processQueue()` and `#emit('next')`, so pendingZero fires before any same-tick `idle` from draining (`test/basic.ts:850-887` asserts `['pendingZero', 'empty', 'idle']`).
- Events are plain `Event`/`CustomEvent`; `#emit(event, detail?)` at `:227-229`.

6. Existing tests/docs stating intended semantics
Docs:
- `readme.md:210-212`: abort-while-waiting removes the task and `.add()` rejects with the abort reason.
- `readme.md:362-388`: `.clear()` removes all waiting tasks; "The promises returned by `.add()` for the removed tasks reject with an `AbortError` ... The removed tasks do not run, and they do not emit the `error` event"; ignored-promise suppression applies to the original `.add()` promise but a `.then()`/`.addAll()` derived promise must be handled.
- `readme.md:270-289`: `onIdle` resolves when `size === 0 && pending === 0`; `onPendingZero` resolves when `pending === 0`.
- `readme.md:594-602`: `empty` event vs `idle` event.
- `readme.md:625-637`: `pendingZero` emitted when running count becomes zero; `next` also emitted on waiting-task abort and `.clear()`.
- `readme.md:894-929`: abort cancels/removes queued task; `clear()` rejects too but does not signal running tasks; aborting a running task rejects `.add()` and frees concurrency slot but does not stop work.
Tests:
- `test/basic.ts:903-940` — `.clear() rejects the removed tasks and lets the running tasks finish`: asserts removed task rejects with `DOMException`, `name === 'AbortError'`, `message === 'The queue was cleared'`; removed work `didRemovedTaskRun === false`; running task resolves `'🦄'`; `completed` has only the running result; `errors` empty.
- `test/basic.ts:942-972` — `.clear() does not cause an unhandled rejection for an ignored promise`, including clear from an `add` listener before the second/third promises exist; asserts `reasons` empty and each promise rejects with `{name: 'AbortError'}`.
- `test/basic.ts:1007-1071` — clear resolves `onEmpty()`/`onIdle()` when nothing running; with a running task emits `empty` but not `idle`.
- `test/basic.ts:437-457` and `:633-640` — `onIdle` waits for drain and resolves `undefined` on empty.
- `test/basic.ts:642-887` — `onPendingZero` semantics: immediate resolve at pending 0, waits while paused with queued items, ignores errors, works after `clear()`, multiple/rapid waiters, high concurrency, ordering vs `empty`/`idle`.
- `test/advanced.ts:320-350` — queued abort rejects with `{name: 'AbortError'}`, size 0; custom reason forwards.
- `test/advanced.ts:352-486` — abort queued task does not unpause, shared controller removes all three, explicit id removes correct task, custom `FifoQueue` removal by id, duplicate id removes the right one.
- `test/advanced.ts:488-527` — queued abort listener cleaned up when the task starts running.
- `test/advanced.ts:529-587` — aborting a queued task emits no `error`/`completed`, still resolves `empty`/`idle`, and `onError` does not reject.
- `test/advanced.ts:590-696` — clear removes queued abort listeners (including shared-signal one-listener case), keeps the running task's shared listener, and lets a later shared-signal abort reject only the running task; cleared queued promises reject with `{name:'AbortError', message:'The queue was cleared'}`.
- `test/advanced.ts:725-761` — running abort listeners cleaned up when jobs complete.
- `test/strict.ts:340-370` — aborted queued task does not execute (3 of 4 execute).
- `test/strict.ts:661-689` — `clear()` cancels the pending strict-mode timeout and the cleared task promise rejects with `{name: 'AbortError'}`.
- `test/rate-limit.ts:497-568` — aborting shared-signal waiting tasks does not start them, emits no `error`, and nothing starts later.
- `test/rate-limit.ts:647-669` — `.clear()` or abort in the same tick resolves `onRateLimitCleared`.
- `test/rate-limit.ts:671-739` — aborting one waiting task lets a different-signal task start at window end; a skipped aborted task does not consume the interval slot.
- `test/rate-limit.ts:836-891` — after `.clear()` in the same window, a new task is rate-limited and runs at window end; cleared promise rejects with name `AbortError`.
- `test/rate-limit.ts:978-1055` — clear on a paused rate-limited queue removes the window-end timer; a `rateLimitCleared` listener adding a task during `.clear()` runs at window end and `empty` is not emitted while it waits.

7. Test conventions/locations
- `package.json:21`: `"test": "xo && node --import=tsx/esm --test --test-timeout=60000 test/*.ts && del-cli dist && tsc && nz"`. Only `test/*.ts` files run; there is no `benchmark_tests/`.
- New tests must go in an existing/new file matching `test/*.ts`.
- Imports seen in test files: `import {test} from 'node:test';` and `import assert from 'node:assert/strict';`, `import PQueue from '../source/index.js';` (`:10` in `test/basic.ts`), helpers from `'./helpers.js'`.
- `test/helpers.ts` exports `flush()`, `tick(t, ms)`, `countTimers()`, and `FifoQueue` (a custom queue ignoring priorities; `enqueue` stores `{run, id}`, `dequeue` shifts, `remove(id)` finds by id).
- Mock-timer tests use `t.mock.timers.enable({apis: ['setTimeout', 'setInterval', 'Date'], now})` (e.g. `test/rate-limit.ts:499`, `test/strict.ts`); `t.mock.timers.tick(...)` plus `flush()`.

verdict:
`clear()`'s body at `source/index.ts:955-961` creates the correct `AbortError` but never calls `waitingTask.reject?.(error)`, so cleared `.add()` promises neither resolve nor reject (the marker `void error; // Regression: cleared promises never settle.`). This violates the documented contract (`readme.md:362-388`), several existing tests (`test/basic.ts:903-972`, `test/advanced.ts:602,634,658,678`, `test/strict.ts:688`, `test/rate-limit.ts:881,1045`), and the suppression logic has nothing to suppress. Queued-abort cancellation, onIdle, and onPendingZero are implemented as documented and are not the broken path; queued abort already removes the queue item (`PriorityQueue.remove(run)` / custom `remove(id)`), rejects with `signal.reason`, does not run the job, and emits no `error`. Running tasks are outside `#waitingTasks`, so a fixed `clear()` must leave them alone; their completion already emits `idle` when queue and pending both hit zero.

reasoning:
The repair belongs in `clear()` only. Before the loop, `error` already exists; inside the loop, each `WaitingTask` has its `reject` populated (registration `waitingTask.reject = reject` at `source/index.ts:737` precedes `#waitingTasks.add`). Add `waitingTask.reject?.(error);` in place of `void error;`, while keeping `stopListeningForAbort?.()`, `isCleared = true`, and `promise?.catch(() => undefined)` so ignored promises remain handled. Do not clear `#waitingTasks` before using `reject`; the current loop is already before `#waitingTasks.clear()`. Keep the early `if (this.#queue.size === 0) return;` so an idle listener calling `clear()` on an already-empty queue cannot recurse, and keep the `#emitEmptyAndIdle()` / `#updateRateLimitState()` / `#emit('next')` ordering. No change is needed to `#startNextTask` or the queued-abort listener for the stated bug, but while editing verify `PriorityQueue.remove`'s `index === this.#head && false` dead branch (`source/priority-queue.ts:113-114`) remains tolerated by lint/typecheck, since it currently produces only a TS Hint, not an error.

test_plan:
- Run the full suite after the fix: `npm test` (that runs `xo`, `node --import=tsx/esm --test --test-timeout=60000 test/*.ts`, then `tsc` and `nz`). Scoped fast iteration: `node --import=tsx/esm --test test/basic.ts test/advanced.ts test/strict.ts test/rate-limit.ts`.
- Existing tests that should flip from failing to passing: `test/basic.ts:903` `.clear() rejects the removed tasks and lets the running tasks finish`; `test/basic.ts:942` unhandled-rejection suppression; `test/advanced.ts:590-696` clear abort-listener + rejection assertions; `test/strict.ts:661` strict clear rejection; `test/rate-limit.ts:836` and `:997` cleared-error assertions.
- Add/extend a focused test in `test/basic.ts`: create `new PQueue({autoStart: false})`, add 3 tasks (capture a `didRun` boolean), call `clear()`, then for each captured `.add()` promise `await assert.rejects(promise, error => error instanceof DOMException && error.name === 'AbortError' && error.message === 'The queue was cleared')`, assert `didRun === false`, `queue.size === 0`, `queue.pending === 0`.
- Add a focused test for running work: `new PQueue({concurrency: 1})`, first task waits on `pDefer`, second is queued; `clear()`; assert first still resolves its value and its `completed` event fires, queued task rejects with `AbortError`, queued function never executes; after resolving first, `onIdle()` resolves.
- Add a focused `onIdle`/`onPendingZero` test for the post-fix boundary: paused queue with queued tasks, `clear()`, await `queue.onIdle()` and `queue.onPendingZero()` (both should resolve `undefined`); with a running task, `clear()` should resolve `onPendingZero()` only after the running task finishes, and emit `empty` before `idle`.
- Test suppressed-unhandled when caller ignores cleared promises exactly as `test/basic.ts:942-972` does (`process.on('unhandledRejection', ...)`, assert collected reasons empty, then `assert.rejects` each, re-arming the handler).

