timeline.detail:
what:
Production scheduler is `PQueue` in `source/index.ts` (default export), with the default priority queue in `source/priority-queue.ts` and the queue contract/option types in `source/queue.ts` / `source/options.ts`. There is no `benchmark_tests/` directory; the only benchmark is root `bench.ts`.

paths:
- `source/index.ts` — PQueue owner: queue field, WaitingTask sidecar, abort registry, promotion, `.clear()`, `onIdle`, `onPendingZero`.
- `source/priority-queue.ts` — default queue storage/enqueue/remove/dequeue.
- `source/queue.ts` — `RunFunction`, `Queue` contract.
- `source/options.ts` — `Options`, `QueueAddOptions`, `TaskOptions` (signal/timeout/priority/id).
- `source/lower-bound.ts` — binary insertion helper.
- `test/helpers.ts` — test `flush`/`tick`/`countTimers`, and `FifoQueue` custom queue.
- `test/basic.ts`, `test/advanced.ts`, `test/strict.ts`, `test/rate-limit.ts`, `test/debug.ts`, `test/validation.ts`, `test/priority-queue.ts`, `test/types.ts`, `test/fixtures/types.ts`.
- `readme.md` — documented semantics.
- `package.json` — test command, `test/*.ts` glob.
- `bench.ts` — only benchmark file; not part of the test glob.

facts:
1. Owner, item structure, enqueue
- `export default class PQueue<...> extends EventTarget` at `source/index.ts:97`.
- Queue slot: `#queue: QueueType` gets `new this.#queueClass()`; fields at `source/index.ts:128` and `:184`. Default `queueClass = PriorityQueue` at `:171`.
- Running-count field `#pending = 0` at `:132`; running-task set `readonly #runningTasks = new Set<{id: string; priority: number; startTime: number; timeout: number | undefined}>()` at `:143-148`.
- Waiting sidecar set: `readonly #waitingTasks = new Set<WaitingTask>()` at `:154`, where `WaitingTask` is (verbatim, `source/index.ts:81-90`):
```ts
type WaitingTask = {
	id?: string;
	priority?: number;
	promise?: Promise<unknown>;
	reject?: (reason: unknown) => void;
	stopListeningForAbort?: () => void;
	isCleared?: boolean;
};
```
- Underlying queue item: in `PriorityQueue`, `enqueue(run, options)` builds `const element = { priority, id, run };` (`source/priority-queue.ts:58-69`). `run` is `RunFunction = () => Promise<unknown>` (`source/queue.ts:1`), but `PQueue.#add` enqueues a bound async wrapper (see item 4).
- Public enqueue entry: `source/index.ts:863-875`:
```ts
add<TaskResultType>(function_: Task<TaskResultType>, options: Partial<EnqueueOptionsType> = {}): Promise<Awaited<TaskResultType>> {
	const waitingTask: WaitingTask = {};
	const promise = this.#add(function_, options, waitingTask);
	waitingTask.promise = promise;
	if (waitingTask.isCleared) {
		promise.catch(() => undefined);
	}
	return promise;
}
```
- Inner enqueue is `async #add<TaskResultType>(function_, options, waitingTask)` at `source/index.ts:613`. It normalizes `options` (adds queue timeout and generated numeric-string id) at `:615-632`, then returns `new Promise<Awaited<TaskResultType>>((resolve, reject) => {...})` at `:634`. The actual queue insert and sidecar registration are at `source/index.ts:735-738`:
```ts
const hasWaitingTasks = this.#queue.size > 0;
this.#queue.enqueue(run, options);
waitingTask.reject = reject;
this.#waitingTasks.add(waitingTask);
```
Then it installs the queued abort listener (`:741-757`), emits `add` (`:759`), and starts work with `#processQueue()` / `#tryToStartAnother()` (`:761-766`).

2. Queued item representation + abort-while-queued behavior
- Queued item representation is two-level: the sidecar `WaitingTask` stores the `.add()` promise and its `reject`, while the actual queued job is the bound `run` function inside the queue (`PriorityQueue` element `{priority, id, run}` or a custom queue's element).
- `run` wrapper begins at `source/index.ts:641`; it is `bindAsyncContext(async () => { ... })`. It is the thing `dequeue()` returns and `#startNextTask` invokes.
- Queued abort listener (`source/index.ts:741-757`):
```ts
if (options.signal) {
	const {signal} = options;
	waitingTask.stopListeningForAbort = this.#onAbort(signal, () => {
		this.#waitingTasks.delete(waitingTask);

		if (this.#queue instanceof PriorityQueue) {
			this.#queue.remove(run);
		} else {
			this.#queue.remove(id);
		}

		reject(signal.reason);
		// Not only one task: at the end of a window, the first start clears the resume timeout, which would start the others.
		this.#processQueue();
		this.#emit('next');
	});
}
```
So aborting a queued task today: removes the job from the underlying queue (`PriorityQueue.remove(run)` with the function reference, or `queue.remove(id)` for custom queues), deletes its sidecar, calls `reject(signal.reason)` (typically `DOMException` name `AbortError` or the custom abort reason), then processes the next task and emits `next`. The queued job does not execute and does not emit `error`.
- Already-aborted signal at add time: rejected before enqueue, no events (`source/index.ts:635-639`).
- Race guard when a queued job is dequeued but its signal aborted before its queued listener ran (`source/index.ts:646-651`):
```ts
if (options.signal?.aborted) {
	this.#rollbackIntervalConsumption();
	reject(options.signal.reason);
	return;
}
```
This is the "skipped" path: it rolls back the interval slot just consumed and does not increment `#pending`.
- Abort while running is separate: `source/index.ts:692-700` races an abort promise against `(async () => function_({signal}))()`; the `#add()` promise rejects on abort/timeout and frees the slot in `finally` (`:713-731`), while the user function may keep running unless it handles `signal`.

3. clear() today
Verbatim implementation `source/index.ts:947-979`:
```ts
clear(): void {
	// Nothing to clear. Emitting `empty` and `idle` here would recurse without end when an `idle` listener calls `.clear()`.
	if (this.#queue.size === 0) {
		return;
	}

	this.#queue = new this.#queueClass();

	const error = new DOMException('The queue was cleared', 'AbortError');
	for (const waitingTask of this.#waitingTasks) {
		waitingTask.stopListeningForAbort?.();
		waitingTask.isCleared = true;
		waitingTask.promise?.catch(() => undefined);
		void error; // Regression: cleared promises never settle.
	}

	this.#waitingTasks.clear();

	// Note: We preserve strict mode rate-limiting state (ticks)
	// because clear() only clears queued tasks, not rate limit history.
	// This ensures that rate limits are still enforced after clearing the queue.

	// Note: We don't clear #runningTasks as those tasks are still running
	// They will be removed when they complete in the finally block

	// Emit events so waiters (onEmpty, onIdle, onSizeLessThan) can resolve. Before the rate-limit update, because `#emitEmptyAndIdle()` clears the interval timer: a `rateLimitCleared` listener that adds a task would otherwise see the timer of the current window, schedule no resume, and the task would never start.
	this.#emitEmptyAndIdle();

	// Force synchronous update since clear() should have immediate effect
	this.#updateRateLimitState();

	this.#emit('next');
}
```
Today clear():
- Replaces the entire queue (`this.#queue = new this.#queueClass()`), so removed queued jobs cannot run.
- Removes queued abort listeners via `stopListeningForAbort?.()`.
- Sets `waitingTask.isCleared = true` and attaches `waitingTask.promise?.catch(() => undefined)` to suppress any future unhandled rejection.
- Does NOT call `waitingTask.reject?.(error)`. The explicit regression marker is `void error; // Regression: cleared promises never settle.` at `source/index.ts:960`. Consequently every cleared `.add()` promise stays pending forever; rejected with nothing.
- Clears `#waitingTasks`.
- Does not touch `#pending` or `#runningTasks`; currently-running tasks continue.
- Calls `#emitEmptyAndIdle()` (`:973`), which emits `empty` and then `idle` only when `#pending === 0`; with running tasks it emits `empty` but not `idle`. Then `#updateRateLimitState()` and `#emit('next')`.

4. running vs queued tracking and promotion
- Queued: `this.#queue.size` (getter `source/index.ts:1145-1147`) plus `#waitingTasks`.
- Running: `#pending` (`source/index.ts:132`, getter `:1179-1181`) plus `#runningTasks` set.
- Dequeue/start is `#startNextTask(): boolean | undefined` at `source/index.ts:511-557`:
```ts
#startNextTask(): boolean | undefined {
	if (this.#queue.size === 0) {
		this.#emitEmptyAndIdle();
		return false;
	}

	if (this.#isPaused) {
		if (!this.#doesIntervalAllowAnother) {
			this.#isIntervalPausedAt(Date.now());
		}
		return false;
	}

	const now = Date.now();
	const canInitializeInterval = !this.#isIntervalPausedAt(now);

	if (this.#doesIntervalAllowAnother && this.#doesConcurrentAllowAnother) {
		const job = this.#queue.dequeue()!;

		if (!this.#isIntervalIgnored) {
			this.#consumeIntervalSlot(now);
			this.#scheduleRateLimitUpdate();
		}

		if (canInitializeInterval) {
			this.#initializeIntervalIfNeeded();
		}

		const pendingBefore = this.#pending;
		job();

		if (this.#pending === pendingBefore) {
			this.#emit('next');
			return;
		}

		this.#emit('active');

		return true;
	}

	return false;
}
```
- The queued-to-running transition happens inside `job()` (the bound `run`, `:641-662`): it first `waitingTask.stopListeningForAbort?.(); this.#waitingTasks.delete(waitingTask);` (`:643-644`), checks already-aborted signal (`:646-651`), then `this.#pending++;` and adds `runningTask = {id, priority, startTime, timeout}` to `#runningTasks` (`:653-662`).
- `#processQueue` (`:601-604`) loops `while (this.#tryToStartAnother()) {}`; `#tryToStartAnother` (`:500-508`) loops while `#startNextTask()` returns `undefined` (aborted skipped task), so it can skip many aborted jobs.
- On task finish/abort/timeout, `finally` (`:718-731`) removes the running task and `queueMicrotask(() => { this.#next(); });`.
- `#next` (`:393-402`):
```ts
#next(): void {
	this.#pending--;

	if (this.#pending === 0) {
		this.#emit('pendingZero');
	}

	this.#processQueue();
	this.#emit('next');
}
```

5. onIdle and onPendingZero definitions/triggers
- `#onEvent` helper, `source/index.ts:231-250` (listens with an `AbortController` and calls `resolve()` with no argument):
```ts
async #onEvent(events: EventName | EventName[], shouldHandle?: () => boolean): Promise<void> {
	const eventList = Array.isArray(events) ? events : [events];

	return new Promise(resolve => {
